#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403954 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f916d57-a48d-5f42-a1e6-2cc8f554dc1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${D}^{-1}$ and $q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403956} {:answer/id #uuid "a2636cd7-9981-5e94-8ce6-ffc7ac605d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${D}^{-1}$ and $t$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403957} {:answer/id #uuid "cd887cdb-1f42-5d46-bc3e-089ab176ac9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D$ and ${I}_{n}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403958} {:answer/id #uuid "e14838e1-6770-514b-a8d3-264fece5dc27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${D}^{-1}$ and $D$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403959} {:answer/id #uuid "45769dbd-ae3d-5976-b913-0ac9c72c7a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D$ and $q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403960}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403956} :answer-field/id #uuid "96efe5ef-8066-599a-a9a3-934df32a5fcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ecf8e0a5-c1dd-5243-9116-de59a516e7ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-78561" :question/problem "Consider a system of $n$ linear equations in $n$ variables, written in matrix form as $D\\,t=q.$ Here, $D$ is the non-singular coefficient matrix of the system, $t$ is the column vector containing the variables, and $q$ is the column vector containing the constants. The unique solution of the system can be found using the method shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,t & =q \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{\\phantom{{D}^{-1}}}}D\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q \\\\ {I}_{n}\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\,\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{\\phantom{\\mathbf{q}}}} \\\\ t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom and left to right, what are the missing parts of the derivation?" :question/worked-solution "Since $D$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${D}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $D\\,t=q$ by ${D}^{-1},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,t & =q \\\\ {D}^{-1}D\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q \\\\ {I}_{n}\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q \\\\ t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts are ${D}^{-1}$ and $q.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ae48163-0998-5b3d-b29e-8d844bd75e73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453454} {:answer/id #uuid "fac7d0b5-081a-56e0-901b-a2ae495d04aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453455}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453454} :answer-field/id #uuid "d31b9f46-6bca-5c00-88d4-25fd24f38387" :answer-field/key "${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454477} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8a92446-e8c8-5739-82ef-d0ba00e62ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453456} {:answer/id #uuid "00c70672-536d-5c0e-84ab-327beb9e056e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453457}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453457} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d6e49be-3808-5326-8880-c9624d3a62b4" :answer-field/key "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\})=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454478} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d46f9ac4-f8c7-5cc1-9675-07387c704c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453458} {:answer/id #uuid "325a6d83-0f48-5758-9374-3733e1e6f1c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453459}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453458} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ea1fc63-07a4-50e6-a7e4-57b3bf645806" :answer-field/key "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6a087057-8a12-50fd-9bce-b13921276b0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-159782" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement “${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$” is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\})=2$ is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\})=2.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=2$ is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=3$ (not $2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\})=2$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410546 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "143b59b0-133f-55a9-aba4-577093e492df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410522} {:answer/id #uuid "47588aca-363b-5cbe-b2d3-66854ec8696e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410523} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6e1323-5cbb-5d05-81d7-ed37a2420f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410524} {:answer/id #uuid "76ef7f6d-ef95-5749-93fb-20eeddd0b606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410526} {:answer/id #uuid "a84f8fed-839c-579a-aec7-743be12c797b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410530}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410530} :answer-field/id #uuid "f77cbe95-6bf7-5129-86d2-ca625369224e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a1e15f70-9949-53c2-8d3b-1dd646d018c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-79311" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}})=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}})=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74a173e3-4894-57a1-b114-4cebc35d441c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410525} {:answer/id #uuid "95748133-fe39-5ce6-8d35-f5c78273de56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410529} {:answer/id #uuid "b88d0552-0930-5b71-a537-c45316717b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410534} {:answer/id #uuid "dba05347-6a57-5e05-bc58-97062bc9c25c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410535} {:answer/id #uuid "f02020d2-3fed-5ef7-a4e4-c92e5db8fab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410540}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410535} :answer-field/id #uuid "620ffecc-6446-571f-9e30-35668717eb9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a83939de-1a77-573b-b113-0b677761ad51" :question/math-academy-id "q-35272" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}})=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}})=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410548 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09726ccb-ffa4-5f18-9b63-38b552326b64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410521} {:answer/id #uuid "84c4b891-3a0a-57b7-8450-2deb8c9ca291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410528} {:answer/id #uuid "afbe1ce8-7c2f-59bd-b55a-918cd14fecdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410532} {:answer/id #uuid "b850f1a8-97df-56f1-9296-e435261baa73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410533} {:answer/id #uuid "eae0e3e4-3d5e-500f-a238-5ead6dabaad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410539}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410539} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6ce72d9-3525-5097-88ee-c623de804b20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f5153a05-4fb4-55bc-add5-e73b5a837403" :question/math-academy-id "q-70556" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 8 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414557 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f2ce482-297e-5123-8b31-697b0b5fac72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414548} {:answer/id #uuid "af4825cd-c5e4-5e8a-8d2b-bc19ba58408d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414549} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a8864f-ea76-50f6-a0e6-8e2da407c587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414550} {:answer/id #uuid "e6107e83-a324-574b-b6b1-55f886aa30a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414552} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c5b820-a95e-5c81-b191-bca183318d28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414553}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414550} :answer-field/id #uuid "600652d9-cb9a-523c-87c3-f7ac3f132c36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "db70d6b5-5ab8-59da-800f-f701ef4ed346" :question/math-academy-id "q-20836" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 7 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 7 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+3z &=1 \\\\ 0x+y+z &=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+3z &=1 \\\\ y+z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ To do this, we apply the back substitution. \n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+z &=1\\,⇒\\,y=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2(1-z)+3z &=1\\,⇒\\,x=-1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-1-z \\\\ y &=1-z \\\\ z &=z\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1-z \\\\ 1-z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "546c8e95-b26b-5ff9-83d6-638b399b687a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{5}{2},y=9,z=-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415684} {:answer/id #uuid "854c87f7-583e-578a-92c5-d3db59792427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5}{2},y=9,z=\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415685} {:answer/id #uuid "92176233-f61a-5a1f-a96a-0e59d129daf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{5}{2},y=-9,z=-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415686} {:answer/id #uuid "922397fb-a6df-5cdc-b3c4-40ed001f749e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-5,y=18,z=-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415687} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d344f8-2327-5d63-96e1-0bdf947cb05c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-5,y=-18,z=-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415688}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415684} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7180735-62b0-5917-9dd2-0f45d066402b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2997dd93-b478-5bad-935c-182c80540138" :question/math-academy-id "q-10091" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y+2z &=0 \\\\ x+2y+3z &=2 \\\\ 3x+y+z &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-1(1-9)+2(1-6) \\\\  & =+8-10 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -8 & -5 \\\\ -1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -8 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -8 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 9 \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=-\\frac{5}{2},y=9,z=-\\frac{9}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417081 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46e61892-168f-50bc-9184-3087f0aa19c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2+z \\\\ -z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417072} {:answer/id #uuid "59f7a4dc-26a3-51be-9f66-57bb37e63b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}z \\\\ 2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417073} {:answer/id #uuid "5e341f4d-4527-53f0-a549-17a212c07c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2+z \\\\ z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417075} {:answer/id #uuid "86d4df82-dbcb-5384-b53a-8ca94675a39a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2+z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417076} {:answer/id #uuid "b3937866-2599-550e-8d7d-230111af4c2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2+z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417077}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417077} :answer-field/id #uuid "40f01e87-54e2-5dd2-9850-20313302577b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "32425960-ceab-536a-8061-1f2469974e99" :question/math-academy-id "q-32220" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z &=2 \\\\ y+2z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-z &=2 \\\\ y+2z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2+z \\\\ y &=-2z \\\\ z &=z,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the latter can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2+z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417082 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0250b550-0b7c-5c05-b8f5-33da7345f93c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417069} {:answer/id #uuid "0e30254f-fef5-5030-ad74-62323657f390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417070} {:answer/id #uuid "32810e7f-ad8d-5b1e-a7d2-839ff11f12a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417071} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0d5bd9-5c97-5445-afdc-58640864c07d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417074} {:answer/id #uuid "e23cbcca-7092-5855-aeeb-dd2de6061a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417078}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417071} :answer-field/id #uuid "26b28742-5ee2-597a-8428-7062724625e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6deb5ca1-ae5b-5721-895d-6efa8ac5fdfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-131730" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y &=12 \\\\ x+ky &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & 2 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & 2 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 12\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2-3k & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+ky &=4 \\\\ (2-3k)y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=\\frac{2}{3},$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+ky=4.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "336cb96e-8d35-5f9f-92e4-bf01f1fe608d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420806} {:answer/id #uuid "5725181a-ff3e-5107-8722-cd79c0a511e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420811} {:answer/id #uuid "b75e75d9-4872-5fbc-a59d-3e54eebab508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420824} {:answer/id #uuid "bb525c0d-2a19-503f-b30d-3b12439ddf16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420825} {:answer/id #uuid "cab0cf74-f931-5eb5-9974-e3db30298fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420829}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420825} :answer-field/id #uuid "75968549-80b9-5200-918a-66a961c78ce2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0b58d794-d6a1-55fe-88d5-660b59bdd30a" :question/math-academy-id "q-42578" :question/problem "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =20-18 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dafe486-15fc-5abc-8a0d-2beb22386f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420809} {:answer/id #uuid "8e814c6a-3786-561d-9f46-38ef12a0585a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420818} {:answer/id #uuid "90c1ce8c-7b18-5377-9a47-ace432f229bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420819} {:answer/id #uuid "cc7cb828-c853-54d8-b205-d802c97b37f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420830} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b62576-113c-5f4d-b29d-3ce30a3f8585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420831}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420819} :answer-field/id #uuid "660640e3-e8ef-509a-a758-3c65a1428537" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "37f105cd-9dd0-500b-bb29-7f9f80a1a14c" :question/math-academy-id "q-43071" :question/problem "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =6-2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420849 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44a67a7f-7ce3-5fc4-9a99-c7b43778cf51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "77" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420810} {:answer/id #uuid "755ff62c-e4b0-56e4-8933-fdd296936d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420815} {:answer/id #uuid "9577d942-a37d-5bb6-ad63-4cff94938a48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420820} {:answer/id #uuid "b74bd5d3-d00b-534f-908c-090b06c3201d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420823} {:answer/id #uuid "c498389f-d133-538a-bef9-88e693ebc92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420828}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420815} :answer-field/id #uuid "633fd2f4-984e-51bc-b51a-5f37a89e06b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "38d108f6-377b-5951-bc6b-4ec43d8b652b" :question/math-academy-id "q-88281" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $det(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that both matrices are triangular. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 2)\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot 3\\cdot (-4)) \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =-72.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420850 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1de3d101-6ebd-5bbc-ad0b-5f1d9ed17bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420803} {:answer/id #uuid "3896dbb3-7bb1-5f7c-b8bf-88d4cf58a131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420808} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f0dcd3-6430-515f-8e85-02e0bf1c5fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420832} {:answer/id #uuid "dd787881-42cf-53fe-9c3e-08b97bcc5ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420834} {:answer/id #uuid "e50a27dc-031b-52dd-8815-f79824db9e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420837}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420803} :answer-field/id #uuid "db7de380-466f-5089-9f2e-e91927307109" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7f0a84be-936b-55f9-b169-54a768974c12" :question/math-academy-id "q-42577" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $det(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the $2$nd column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot 1(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (8-6) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420851 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "256655f5-3716-5c72-b038-769fa376cf93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420804} {:answer/id #uuid "65294e4c-a8db-5c12-befc-326f1199fa33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420812} {:answer/id #uuid "6c92f4c3-474c-5da4-bd3e-a7ddf9353c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,700" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420813} {:answer/id #uuid "b440a564-08a1-5c84-a502-ec561384a59f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420821} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f48478-6c84-5d48-9002-c26925eb220f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420836}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420804} :answer-field/id #uuid "db1d1fba-e4ba-50df-bd35-d24e24ca86ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "80589b9c-bfb0-57bc-8e70-de307d877343" :question/math-academy-id "q-88306" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 270 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $2$nd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 270 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300+0 & 250+20 & 100+0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300+0 & 250+20 & 100+0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 0 & 20 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $2$nd row, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 0 & 20 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =0+20(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}300 & 100 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =20\\cdot (300\\cdot 1-100\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =20\\cdot 200 \\\\  & =4\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420852 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f66b246-867d-5382-b745-224f4b0b1960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-276" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420805} {:answer/id #uuid "6f34b981-25b3-5127-9dcc-d184355dec85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "429" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420814} {:answer/id #uuid "bbaccfd8-0fee-5b02-b497-78e555afcf98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "294" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420826} {:answer/id #uuid "dd980cfa-ae7f-54ba-bba4-12f039277176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-372" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420835} {:answer/id #uuid "e9605807-1150-5819-807b-86c3f17e6ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-346" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420838}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420835} :answer-field/id #uuid "96ea088a-9b87-51d6-9259-b1c59aec445a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8c3dc4f1-ad56-595b-a7eb-41a85271c7fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-130693" :question/problem "Find\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 114 & 210 & 198 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $2$nd row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 114 & 210 & 198 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 113+1 & 210+0 & 198+0 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 113+1 & 210+0 & 198+0 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $2$nd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{vmatrix} & =0+1(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 210 & 198\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(4\\cdot 198-2\\cdot 210) \\\\  & =-(792-420) \\\\  & =-372\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15bb6772-ebfa-5300-9707-f0c44ba3ca38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420801} {:answer/id #uuid "1657176b-b7bb-5bac-8681-50357708185d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420802} {:answer/id #uuid "341db512-89dd-5d60-9521-449fcc83f4d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420807} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecdfc0d-790a-509f-8041-8c18f54a719a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420817} {:answer/id #uuid "bd130b49-aec7-5947-9ffc-20dcb30a8a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420827}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420827} :answer-field/id #uuid "a81cf8c4-10bc-54bc-9308-65fe3b30e2ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9e9ddda3-97aa-551d-9453-cae76713ec59" :question/math-academy-id "q-43070" :question/problem "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =10-5 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420854 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a0c86ff-0d80-5008-95a2-bec3a1c1a808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420799} {:answer/id #uuid "11030227-d4ce-5b70-8894-186891176d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420800} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd0e1b8-d837-556e-96c2-a100f45e8fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420816} {:answer/id #uuid "b4fdf14b-3a08-5a96-9420-377d1a8d1538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420822} {:answer/id #uuid "daea1037-0203-5328-a357-a7d37817bb29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420833}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420816} :answer-field/id #uuid "57142131-309b-5e63-93f0-085dd8ef09bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b1079762-fa86-56ef-a06d-de94245b17fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-130474" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $det(C).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(C)=det\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the $2$nd column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot (-1)(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(6-4)\\cdot (-1)(12-5) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c8fc809-c7c8-51da-97a9-cd29d45be4a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421534} {:answer/id #uuid "35223ea7-957d-558a-abed-700d6db7d746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421535} {:answer/id #uuid "3f394fb3-e71c-5ca8-9f2c-6be2526c01f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421536} {:answer/id #uuid "60dea114-5917-5702-91f4-2d9dcc51d500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421537} {:answer/id #uuid "d7328dc6-408b-5e04-a91b-4f561c57f94a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421542}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421535} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd431940-6ae8-53c2-934a-5aaba85bb3f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e78f486a-816c-52d1-9895-59ae0c720ab7" :question/math-academy-id "q-130635" :question/problem "If $B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${B}^{5}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${B}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${B}^{2}=B,$ so $B$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${B}^{5}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{5} & ={B}^{2}\\cdot {B}^{2}\\cdot B \\\\  & =B\\cdot B\\cdot B \\\\  & ={B}^{2}\\cdot B \\\\  & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & ={B}^{2} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{B}^{5}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424594 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a113c4d-a0ac-5651-906e-41d84f5d836e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424582} {:answer/id #uuid "5368a6fd-1c3c-5ec5-98c7-f3629d82df88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424583} {:answer/id #uuid "78554db1-b432-51b4-936b-3b8941909583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424585} {:answer/id #uuid "a841feda-cb6b-5b98-990a-d1a0abf6cfb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424588} {:answer/id #uuid "ff9dadc2-a499-5eb3-a904-a3da6ea8a443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424591}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424588} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fd465c3-4977-5d35-b197-a5e9f9656a35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4780154a-e6c5-5212-9585-41f4b2c7d38a" :question/math-academy-id "q-34832" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\})=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 & 15 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424595 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73c49f7c-a1f3-56e9-95d3-4e8d7959ede8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424584} {:answer/id #uuid "93ff253e-bb05-5ca6-8757-db5007d7fe23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424586} {:answer/id #uuid "9b73c0f1-2d21-5d2b-981e-9083bbda05f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424587} {:answer/id #uuid "bc38effb-2332-5570-8872-70a5b22fb92e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424589} {:answer/id #uuid "cbcba230-afee-51b7-a5c4-70037d514241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424590}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424590} :answer-field/id #uuid "29131f28-d633-5715-a931-1a4a0aae454c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f427e4e2-13a5-5d7b-8b8d-103e6b378823" :question/math-academy-id "q-79320" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17602323-8777-51fe-9a70-fac173438e8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425938} {:answer/id #uuid "3745dd5b-bc8f-5c5f-bfbb-0a70d0ee8eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425940} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1862b9-030a-5755-b668-e59ca4c9e456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425944} {:answer/id #uuid "a1294db0-0da9-5a02-b9e5-791ef34dcf11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425946} {:answer/id #uuid "cf6dc1f0-4ead-53bf-8e9f-9b57805aa51d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425947}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425947} :answer-field/id #uuid "80130b9b-78a0-54cc-bf84-e036ce6c2ff0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "db005b4e-86cd-5194-b2fd-10ca4ee59c03" :question/math-academy-id "q-42021" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=3 \\\\ -3x-(k+1)y &=-9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -(k+1) & -9\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -(k+1) & -9\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -k+5 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1-2y &=3 \\\\ -(k-5)y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=5,$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+2y=3.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426755 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01601b6f-c05d-574c-9a4a-d776d7830de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=3,\\,b=-1,\\,c=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426727} {:answer/id #uuid "4d9e88a0-7c5e-5e8c-9e9a-14296af074a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=2,\\,b=-1,\\,c=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426732} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f35897-b61a-57d6-81d6-9ea4cf4754e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=-2,\\,b=1,\\,c=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426735} {:answer/id #uuid "df865a24-76e9-5e0c-aef0-ccb73198892d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=-3,\\,b=1,\\,c=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426739} {:answer/id #uuid "f375eeb9-cae0-5b47-8162-0ab390a8e5ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=1,\\,b=3,\\,c=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426742}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426727} :answer-field/id #uuid "456a058b-2a76-50e4-b196-28caf276666d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "06d7e42a-14b7-5ba4-9656-a3c1d06d273b" :question/math-academy-id "q-10006" :question/problem "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-z &=0 \\\\ x+y+3z &=2 \\\\ 3x-2y+z &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & b \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ a & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ c \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat are the values of $a,b$ and $c?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+0y-z &=0 \\\\ x+y+3z &=2 \\\\ 3x-2y+z &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=3,\\,b=-1,\\,c=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426756 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f600605e-c432-5a49-8937-87085525ad30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426743}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426743} :answer-field/id #uuid "28353e39-acc8-5471-9aa6-69d0698d44c5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426745} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1bb35e7-e173-5077-9b70-1eca4b1960a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426740}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426740} :answer-field/id #uuid "858e4e65-7369-5bb6-9760-59c91beb24f5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426752} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e1d1b7d-645c-539e-8352-b3dfb9ffcd03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426728}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426728} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0491ce2-8d7c-51ed-99a4-858a23cff123" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426753} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b7db70e-4c06-5847-ae4c-8d2d12c80e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426730}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426730} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7de047a-a7eb-5fa7-82bc-f250f9e7cda3" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5bfaecd0-980e-55a7-befc-289a7a9c5c34" :question/math-academy-id "q-317636" :question/problem "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2z+3x-5 &=4 \\\\ 6z-4y+3 &=15 \\\\ 7y+2x-2 &=11\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n|  |  | $3$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} |  |  |  | $x$ |  |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} | $-4$ | $6$ |  |  |  | $y$ |  | $=$ |  | $12$ |  | . |\n|  |  | $2$ | {{field-4}} | $0$ |  |  |  | $z$ |  |  |  | $13$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+0y+2z &=9 \\\\ 0x-4y+6z &=12 \\\\ 2x+7y+0z &=13\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426757 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8600538f-009c-502c-8f93-c0f999bc2b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426734}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426734} :answer-field/id #uuid "3858435e-6d82-5994-86f0-620119922670" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426746} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11aba24b-9268-590e-8810-5c57e7e3c410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426729}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426729} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dca1250-a568-5b14-aa99-ede3c01994fe" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426747} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3575d9f-b5c1-5e4f-8e61-3fb7541222f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426738}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426738} :answer-field/id #uuid "58b5d536-1639-5442-8db5-05c5714eee02" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426749} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eefcde1b-d3b8-55ec-9bff-1b6b1e19f657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426741}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426741} :answer-field/id #uuid "75499a87-c853-5d3b-8493-31cc1387404a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "87b42522-bb27-5dd3-ac3e-bd3279e82fdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-317519" :question/problem "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8y+6x-3z+4 &=0 \\\\ 5z-3y+x-6 &=0 \\\\ 7x-4z-y-9 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | $8$ | $-3$ |  |  |  | $x$ |  |  |  | $-4$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $1$ | $-3$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |  | $y$ |  | $=$ |  | {{field-3}} |  | . |\n|  |  | {{field-4}} | $-1$ | $-4$ |  |  |  | $z$ |  |  |  | $9$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+8y-3z &=-4 \\\\ x-3y+5z &=6 \\\\ 7x-y-4z &=9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426758 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "223847b6-00fc-5235-8827-7e678d11053d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=1,\\,b=-3,\\,c=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426731} {:answer/id #uuid "62f10e81-bf0b-5d8f-abd5-66b9009b22b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=1,\\,b=-3,\\,c=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426733} {:answer/id #uuid "ca5cbda7-94a5-56d2-8e39-bf95cd46be03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=5,\\,b=0,\\,c=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426736} {:answer/id #uuid "ccaa4421-7462-521e-bd86-4c7357d0d8ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=0,\\,b=-1,\\,c=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426737} {:answer/id #uuid "fc3f9e0f-2358-5b78-8d1f-f66161800384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=0,\\,b=-4,\\,c=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426744}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426737} :answer-field/id #uuid "83a4688c-a103-5974-9014-77e44fbfd4ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "da13acd8-77ae-5f2b-ba9c-504353353d52" :question/math-academy-id "q-36179" :question/problem "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+5z &=5 \\\\ -x+5y-3z &=2 \\\\ x-4y+7z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & a & 5 \\\\ b & 5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat are the values of $a,b$ and $c?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+0y+5z &=5 \\\\ -x+5y-3z &=2 \\\\ x-4y+7z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=0,\\,b=-1,\\,c=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430827 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4aa840c-676d-579d-907a-45c66e348cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454000} {:answer/id #uuid "5dfd99bd-c5b6-5c79-9329-4d49aaf5514c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454001}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454001} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1077dc8-5cdf-55a4-9cf4-9f78c96894c1" :answer-field/key "${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454750} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b075b0e-71d3-5492-a58e-181a0bd4a531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454002} {:answer/id #uuid "00552208-c5f0-56e1-9eb5-0d86c6ef59df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454003}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454002} :answer-field/id #uuid "56ff5afd-db04-5235-86fe-8c0c85c46c31" :answer-field/key "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454751} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aee0c146-1417-5ad8-bd81-8ddd4be82e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454004} {:answer/id #uuid "80051909-7b26-58b5-b6ce-f8a75cd2c9ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454005}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454005} :answer-field/id #uuid "d98cc577-1407-5f32-81d7-2d8910452f11" :answer-field/key "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\})=3$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "48a8881c-e6ac-54a2-9375-24bf5efbb3a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79325" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement “${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$” is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ Moreover, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ are linearly independent vectors. So,  ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=2$ is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=3$ (not $2$).\n\n- Statement $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\})=3$ is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\})=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$”; $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\})=3$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430828 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e5d85dd-4d17-573a-8b74-42d7cabad2c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430810} {:answer/id #uuid "6c7cbc7a-dd9b-537a-83c0-ac5cd300c350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430811} {:answer/id #uuid "86142c8b-886b-5e6e-9f79-4adb8275d8d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430812} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff06e75-fb08-5fe4-bd16-8b0cce240878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430816} {:answer/id #uuid "ae48cc0a-d8a5-5c9e-8d7b-c02523c38386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430818}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430812} :answer-field/id #uuid "a08b0b02-a55d-50fe-a6cc-f8c71f19c1af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6f3e3a92-ac43-52ed-a6de-e1847c3b16d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-35300" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 6 & -9 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430829 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cee02f8-68cf-5851-8f1a-97dfedd2201f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430814} {:answer/id #uuid "9eeeff2c-a26a-5efe-999b-770dee902f46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430815} {:answer/id #uuid "a027a479-344f-5843-8902-bf22751ea32f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430817} {:answer/id #uuid "bd58722b-d78b-5b4d-ac72-d93c0e1f07e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430819} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d9e5de-adc7-5e5d-8e0c-0e55b15b1b69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430820}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430820} :answer-field/id #uuid "1068104a-c084-560a-bbeb-c3ccd952752a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fd38fa95-000b-54ff-9226-2d221d8be579" :question/math-academy-id "q-79321" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "039d5f33-dfa0-51c2-93be-2b7000137d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435129} {:answer/id #uuid "0597b830-4974-5289-8d3c-e5ddb2d894f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435130} {:answer/id #uuid "274c794a-3cbb-5dc2-bbb2-1f1131f70f1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435131} {:answer/id #uuid "28126022-8f20-5f62-8417-0685db76f23b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435132} {:answer/id #uuid "3b9511ec-b4f9-5c7b-b596-beebd0bcb054" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435134}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435132} :answer-field/id #uuid "d554649a-905d-57c6-af27-cc40c4c45a3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0807a9e2-ba3b-5d6e-8562-a580bc61f62a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104515" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y-2z &=-1 \\\\ 3x-z &=5+3y \\\\ 3z-2y &=6-x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind  $x+y+z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system in the correct order:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y-2z &=-1 \\\\ 3x-3y-z &=5 \\\\ x-2y+3z &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, we  write the system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-9-2)-(9+1)-2(-6+3) \\\\  & =-22-10+6 \\\\  & =-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 10 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 8 & -5 \\\\ -7 & 4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & -10 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 5 \\\\ -7 & -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 1 & -7 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 1 & -7 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 1 & -7 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}-26 \\\\ 26 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x+y+z=1-1+1=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435148 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a64a01b-e02d-535d-a027-6746b0121352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=A{B}^{-1}\\,d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435133} {:answer/id #uuid "7539810a-78b4-56d6-8b1e-ddb5c34343c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=A\\,d\\,B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435136} {:answer/id #uuid "8afcf5b9-6023-5abe-9e32-15e5a0353825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x={B}^{-1}A\\,d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435137} {:answer/id #uuid "b14cf1e4-785f-5b17-867e-45e5d7cc5a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=BA\\,d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435138} {:answer/id #uuid "e12ef937-d966-5968-a2bc-5cb307c85739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=A\\,d\\,{B}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435141}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435137} :answer-field/id #uuid "3722cf83-66cd-54b2-a5b1-3a7fa87b1c56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0c58ffa5-e4d4-5bf5-8b35-8a71f48534ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-78565" :question/problem "Consider a matrix equation $B\\,x=A\\,d,$ where $A$ and $B$ are $2\\times 2$ non-singular matrices, while $x$ and $d$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors. Solve the equation for $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${B}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $B\\,x=A\\,d$ by ${B}^{-1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B\\,x & =A\\,d \\\\ {B}^{-1}B\\,x & ={B}^{-1}A\\,d \\\\ {I}_{2}\\,x & ={B}^{-1}A\\,d \\\\ x & ={B}^{-1}A\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435149 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fc306a8-65be-5769-9f23-972df29a47cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435135} {:answer/id #uuid "b90c4ae0-c1ef-52b6-b3de-1cbcb9e94c5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435139} {:answer/id #uuid "d60be700-46fb-5903-a3f8-cfb1794eaf06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435140} {:answer/id #uuid "ed96fe73-34bc-5554-8fe0-b110d7f3f6b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435142} {:answer/id #uuid "f3dc6b18-74c4-595b-92c7-e48fa955a38e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435143}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435142} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0e50419-c826-5a89-9337-910bb9a469c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e321af2f-d67a-5dc4-a077-c6060845ea99" :question/math-academy-id "q-78553" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+z &=3 \\\\ x-2y+z &=1 \\\\ 2x-y+2z &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=-1,y=1,z=4.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=-1+1+4=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435767 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fd88020-b9de-5670-bbb4-d000faac032e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "176" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435744} {:answer/id #uuid "4e375b53-cb2c-56d7-a604-46a74124343b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "214" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435749} {:answer/id #uuid "82e7b3e3-84d8-5737-a5f1-d3862a09a444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "186" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435753} {:answer/id #uuid "ac3611ee-496d-5cec-8534-0299f1c2f846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "260" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435756} {:answer/id #uuid "f62fda4e-f88c-56fa-9e53-1860b2a19543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "244" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435762}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435756} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e7b949d-cbe6-5045-bf34-26b00edf4f82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3797960f-4f46-583b-afe6-1bb724ee5f4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-130641" :question/problem "Find\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 103 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $2$nd row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 103 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 144+0 & 158+0 & 102+1 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 144+0 & 158+0 & 102+1 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $2$nd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{vmatrix} & =0+1(-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 144 & 158\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(2\\cdot 158-4\\cdot 144) \\\\  & =-(316-576) \\\\  & =260\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435768 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74feda84-0372-5ba8-a03f-6504fdd43cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435751} {:answer/id #uuid "81e999d9-b74a-531e-b396-75bcda8940a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435752} {:answer/id #uuid "a5465cc4-707e-5513-9c9b-2c4ccb77d7a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435754} {:answer/id #uuid "aee93a59-f631-5538-b707-3dbdac3a5bb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435757} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c0bd69-0ae8-5c67-b00c-53d8d88f988d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435758}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435752} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea1881ff-14ad-5797-8c35-e1990ea0829e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "788f9347-638e-5e07-bc97-727179291d72" :question/math-academy-id "q-130780" :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=3,\n$$\nfind \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}a-2 & b & c \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a-2 & b & c \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}a-2 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a-2 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}}_{3}+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3-2(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3-2\\cdot (5+1) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435769 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d19261f-2d17-5ce6-a528-357db19c92fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{76}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435743} {:answer/id #uuid "44e2e6b4-ad39-5370-b46a-da247adce9ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{82}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435745} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc3f9d6-53b9-5b49-91f9-095f7560a365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{84}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435748} {:answer/id #uuid "505a34ac-ba5d-5a72-8645-7ed3b579499e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435750} {:answer/id #uuid "ce24b16c-5b82-5027-a711-29aa725ec9c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435760}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435748} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2f79099-495d-5b97-91da-18a1c01e2ef1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8501cdd8-7879-5e81-a6ba-962c1d7f9129" :question/math-academy-id "q-130532" :question/problem "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $2$nd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+1(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+2(-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-18+16)+(-30-4)-2(20+3) \\\\  & =-4-34-46 \\\\  & =-84\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}=-\\frac{1}{84}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435770 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44f82cc2-edfd-5f43-b0c8-b4fb5bfe4f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435746} {:answer/id #uuid "48f7dc16-cbeb-5e5b-b98b-3d2a1113c52c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435747} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ea38b1-c61b-5047-b0ee-8b524e948890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435755} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3a92c4-d4e6-5591-8bf1-5151ac26a42e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435759} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f0546b-c6ef-592b-ad4b-1acba6597c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435761}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435747} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9759cd8-4862-5dd3-9edc-5966b55e2eb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "eee0d247-b841-57bb-b4de-7b3606da042f" :question/math-academy-id "q-42583" :question/problem "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $2$nd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+2(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-1(-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 6 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(12-15)+2(14+15)+(-35-30) \\\\  & =3+58-65 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"}]}
